The browser build: NetHack's tty port compiled to WebAssembly
=============================================================

wasm/build.sh PLAYGROUND [OUT] builds it with emscripten (CI pins the
version; see .github/workflows/ci.yml, job "wasm"), from the game's own
sources and the data files (nhdat, symbols, license) of a native install
of the same commit, so the same seed makes the same dungeon: CI compares
its layout hashes with every native build's. A v* tag's release carries
it as nethack-<tag>-wasm.tar.gz: nethack.mjs, nethack.wasm, nethack.data
and BUILD (the commit).

Save files. The C code sees 64-bit longs and pointers (emscripten's
MEMORY64=2, lowered to ordinary wasm32, so it runs in every browser):
the data model of the Linux x86_64 and aarch64 builds, so their save
files, snapshots included (Seeding B15), restore here as they are. A save
restores only in the same version, as everywhere (Seeding B7).

Running it. nethack.mjs is an ES module whose default export is the
emscripten factory. A page passes it:

  locateFile(name)  where nethack.wasm and nethack.data are, such as
                    new URL(name, <the module's URL>).href (emscripten
                    finds the data file from the page's URL otherwise)
  nhKey()           a promise of the next key's byte, or -1 when the
                    terminal is gone; the game waits on it for every key
                    (wasm/wasmtty.c), and pauses for display effects
                    with emscripten_sleep(), both yielding to the page
  preRun            [m => { ... }]: set m.ENV (TERM=xterm, USER,
                    NETHACKOPTIONS) and take the output by replacing
                    m.TTY.default_tty_ops.put_char(tty, byte) and fsync

and then, with the factory's promise resolved, writes what the game
should start from to m.FS and calls m.callMain(args):

  a save            /nethack/save/0<name>.gz (gzipped, as the native
                    builds write them; the browser's sysconf has
                    CHECK_SAVE_UID=0), then callMain(['-u', name])
  a seed            append SEED=<seed> to /nethack/sysconf, as a race's
                    launcher does (the seed stays hidden), then
                    callMain(['-u', name, '-@']) for the seed's character

The output is an 80x24 terminal's: ANSI sequences without termcap
(include/tcap.h). Before each map cell it draws, the game names the
cell's glyph in a private sequence a terminal ignores,
ESC [ ? <glyph> ; <glyph flags> ; <colour> ; <character> z, so a page can
draw tiles over the map (win/tty/wintty.c). The game exits (callMain
returns, onExit runs) when it ends or the player saves.

wasm/test.mjs checks a build under Node: the layout hashes of a seed on
standard input, and the restore of a native build's save.
